WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.710
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.479
and I'm a musician in residence at

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.399
Yale University's Fortunoff Video

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.560
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.796
This video series is designed as a

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.242
companion to our album Where

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.069
Is Our Homeland, which is the first

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.589
volume in the Archive's ongoing

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.640
effort to present songs

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.238
and poems performed by survivors

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.389
in testimonies given to the

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.909
Fortunoff archive over the last

00:00:58.490 --> 00:00:59.540
40 years.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.580
You can learn more about the project

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:02.629
on the Archive's Website

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.320
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.709 --> 00:01:07.819
"Where is our Homeland?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.139
Songs from Testimonies.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.267
Vol. 1" was recorded in

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.820
2019 by the Zisl

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.630
Slepovitch Ensemble, featuring

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.846
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.981 --> 00:01:20.090
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.099
Camp on the accordion, keyboards

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.710
and the guitar, Dmitry Ishenko

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.209
on the bass, and myself on

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:27.210
woodwinds.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.369
The songs in this collection differ

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.109
vastly in terms of style,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.119
social and historical background,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.430
language, and many other aspects.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.559
But collectively, they create

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.981
a multidimensional picture of

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.901
Jewish life before and during

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.640
World War Two.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.339
Peretz H.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.110
was born in 1927

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.309
in Warsaw.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.927
He was a child when the war broke

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.200
out.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.210
He used to sing in the streets

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.150
of Warsaw to earn a living,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.309
and he shared several songs

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.319
in his 1993

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.520
Fortunoff video archive testimony.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.810
One of them is Walc François,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.519
Waltz François, composed

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.379
in 1905 by

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.660
Adam Józef Karasiński

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.242
to lyrics of Andrzej Włast.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.779
That song was a salon waltz

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.158
that enjoyed enormous popularity

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.639
in Poland in the first half

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.779
of the 20th century.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.599
There is no surprise that a song

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.729
written in what originally was

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.069
a sentimentalism era salon

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:32.687
genre, with the lyrics expressing

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.960
the longing for the Golden Times,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.300
Golden Era of the past

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.789
gained such enormous popularity.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.310
Walc François ended up

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.350
among many songs that

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.380
Peretz H.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.919
was singing for his livelihood

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:49.920
during the war.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.740
In my grandma's old music.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.020
This waltz has survived.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.660
I remember an evening at her place.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.580
I see it as in a dream

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.300
A player at the piano.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.980
Guests are gathering in a circle.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.300
Ladies wear white puffs and bustles.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.300
A chrysanthemum in each gentleman's
lapel.

00:03:32.343 --> 00:03:36.260
The magic of yesteryear's memories.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.900
The grace of elegant couples.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.060
Muslin dresses flying like fog.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.180
To the latest Waltz François.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.420
If only, once again.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.620
That beautiful time would return.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.100
If only it would evoke in these
hearts of evil.

